; CLIFF 1.1 — Contextual Localization Integrated File Format
; Normative ABNF grammar (RFC 5234; case-sensitive string literals per RFC 7405).
;
; Changes from CLIFF 1.0:
;   * "name" is relaxed to the character set A-Z a-z 0-9 "_" "-" (no dot).
;   * "tag-name" is introduced: fixed-vocabulary tags stay lowercase kebab-case.
;   * An optional single trailing "," or ";" terminator is accepted on every
;     line (see "terminator" below and spec 5.6).
; Every CLIFF 1.0 document remains a valid CLIFF 1.1 document: the change is a
; pure relaxation, and no production was removed or narrowed.
;
; LEXICAL NOTE
; ------------
; The grammar below operates after a lexical pre-pass that:
;   * decodes UTF-8 (an optional leading U+FEFF BOM is removed),
;   * normalizes CRLF to LF (a bare CR is invalid),
;   * appends a final LF when the stream does not end with one, so that every
;     line production below is LF-terminated,
;   * removes a single optional trailing terminator ("," / ";" then optional
;     SP / HTAB) from the end of a line. The terminator is recognized only
;     after the line's closing quote or bracket has been consumed, so a comma
;     or semicolon inside a quoted string is payload, never a terminator. More
;     than one terminator is a syntax error,
;   * removes blank lines (lines containing only SP / HTAB),
;   * removes comment lines (optional SP / HTAB followed by "#").
; Comments, blank lines, and terminators may therefore appear between any two
; grammar productions, but never inside a quoted string. Their removal is
; transparent everywhere except one place: a blank or comment line terminates
; the continuation of the preceding field, so a parser MUST record that
; boundary before discarding the line (see the semantic constraints below).
; Terminator removal is transparent everywhere and MUST NOT be reported as a
; repair by any parser.
;
; This is the TOLERANT grammar: surrounding whitespace, both ":" and "=", and a
; single trailing terminator are accepted so the format is pleasant to read and
; type by hand. The CANONICAL form is fixed in spec §Serialization: "key:
; value" with exactly one space after the colon, section and entry lines at
; column 0 with no trailing whitespace, and no terminator.
;
; All non-ASCII Unicode scalar values are permitted in strings and comments.
; They are not enumerated here because RFC 5234 char-val ranges are
; US-ASCII-oriented; parsers apply the restrictions in spec §Lexical
; Structure.

cliff-file            = version-line *header-line *section
version-line         = *WSP %s"CLIFF" SP ( %s"1.0" / %s"1.1" ) *WSP [ terminator ] *WSP LF
                      ; both "CLIFF 1.0" and "CLIFF 1.1" are accepted by a 1.1
                      ; implementation; the canonical spelling is "CLIFF 1.1".
                      ; A minor version other than 1.0 / 1.1 (and any other
                      ; major version) MUST be rejected with the supported
                      ; versions named in the diagnostic.

header-line          = field-line
section              = section-line *group-metadata-line *entry

section-line         = *WSP "[" group-path "]" *WSP [ terminator ] *WSP LF
group-path           = name *( "." name )
                      ; "." separates path segments, which is why a name MUST
                      ; NOT contain "." (spec 5.5).

group-metadata-line  = field-line
entry                = entry-line *field-line
entry-line           = *WSP "<" name ">" *WSP [ terminator ] *WSP LF
                      ; a label on one line: the entry it labels runs to the next
                      ; entry line, the next section line, or the end of the file,
                      ; and inside strings "<" and ">" are ordinary payload
                      ; characters. The entry id is a name: uppercase, digits, "_"
                      ; and "-" are permitted and case is significant.

field-line           = *WSP field *WSP [ terminator ] *WSP LF *continuation-line
continuation-line    = *WSP ( string-value / list ) *WSP [ terminator ] *WSP LF
                      ; attaches to the immediately preceding field: string
                      ; fragments only after scalar string fields, list
                      ; fragments only after list fields.
field                = key *WSP ( "=" / ":" ) *WSP value
value                = string-value / list / integer / name / language-tag
string-value         = string *( *WSP string )
                      ; adjacent strings concatenate verbatim (C rules) and
                      ; are allowed on any scalar string field.
list                 = "[" *WSP [ list-items ] *WSP "]"
list-items           = list-item *( *WSP "," *WSP list-item ) *WSP [ "," ]
list-item            = string / integer / name / language-tag
                      ; a list is single-line and never nests another list.

key                  = name
                      ; A key is a bare name, so a quoted key is a syntax error
                      ; rather than a rule this grammar has to state: a name holds
                      ; no character that quoting would protect. The spec says
                      ; nothing else about it either - 6.1 states rules only where
                      ; the grammar leaves a gap (a quoted *value* is well formed,
                      ; which is why C.2.3 must exist; a quoted key is not).
                      ; Tolerant parsing does not change this rule (spec C.1). A
                      ; tolerant parser MAY accept a quoted key as the documented
                      ; relaxation of spec C.2.7, in which case it removes the
                      ; quotes, reports a name-quote repair, and then applies this
                      ; rule to the enclosed text unchanged: a word that is not a
                      ; legal key in that scope stays an unknown-key error (spec
                      ; C.5).

; Names: the relaxed CLIFF 1.1 identifier used for keys, entry ids, group path
; segments, and the namespace / clan header values. The character set is
; exactly ASCII letters, digits, underscore, and hyphen. Uppercase and
; lowercase are both permitted and are case-sensitive; a leading digit,
; hyphen, or underscore is permitted; "." is forbidden because it separates
; group path segments and canonical ID components. Identifiers MUST NOT be
; case-folded or otherwise rewritten by a parser (spec 5.5, 10.1).
name                 = name-char *name-char
name-char            = ALPHA / DIGIT / "_" / "-"

; Tags: the values of the fixed-vocabulary fields type, emotion, status, and
; variant. Tags are NOT relaxed by 1.1: they stay lowercase kebab-case, and
; each is drawn from a closed set (spec 12, 13). A differently-cased spelling
; of a listed value is a vocabulary error, not an alias.
tag-name             = %x61-7A *( %x61-7A / DIGIT / "-" )

; A single optional line terminator, accepted on every line kind and never
; meaningful (spec 5.6). It is recognized only after the line's closing quote
; or bracket; more than one is a syntax error. A canonical serializer MUST NOT
; emit it.
terminator           = "," / ";"

integer              = 1*DIGIT

; BCP 47 language tag. The full BCP 47 grammar is defined by RFC 5646 and is
; normative; this production is a permissive envelope for ABNF completeness.
; Comparison is case-insensitive per BCP 47.
language-tag         = ALPHA *( ALPHA / DIGIT / "-" )

string               = double-string / single-string
double-string        = DQUOTE *( double-char / double-escape ) DQUOTE
double-char          = %x20-21 / %x23-5B / %x5D-7E / non-ascii
                      ; excludes DQUOTE (%x22), backslash (%x5C), controls.
                      ; "," (%x2C) and ";" (%x3B) are ordinary payload here.
double-escape        = "\" ( DQUOTE / "\" / "n" / "r" / "t" )
single-string        = SQUOTE *( single-char / single-escape ) SQUOTE
single-char          = %x20-26 / %x28-5B / %x5D-7E / non-ascii
                      ; excludes SQUOTE (%x27), backslash (%x5C), controls.
single-escape        = "\" ( SQUOTE / "\" / "n" / "r" / "t" )
non-ascii            = %x80-F4
                      ; practical envelope; parsers MUST validate UTF-8
                      ; scalar values per the specification.

WSP                  = SP / HTAB
SP                   = %x20
HTAB                 = %x09
DQUOTE               = %x22
SQUOTE               = %x27
LF                   = %x0A
ALPHA                = %x41-5A / %x61-7A
DIGIT                = %x30-39

; Semantic constraints (enforced by validators, not by ABNF):
;  * version-line must be the first non-ignored line and must declare 1.0 or
;    1.1; any other version is rejected with the supported versions named.
;  * Header fields occur before the first section-line; group metadata lines
;    occur only before the first entry-line of a section.
;  * namespace, clan, source-language, and target-language are all required.
;    A layout (folder "<target-language>/<clan>.cliff" or flat
;    "<clan>.<target-language>.cliff") that disagrees with the header is a
;    WARNING by default in 1.1 (spec 11.3); an implementation MAY offer an
;    explicit opt-in mode that reports it as an error.
;  * Entry-level: source, type (direct or inherited), and status are
;    required; target is optional and required only for status translated /
;    reviewed / final (status initial allows a missing target).
;  * Section paths and entry ids are unique; duplicate entry ids are a hard
;    error (never last-wins). Canonical IDs
;    namespace.clan.group-path.entry-id are unique. Case is significant, so
;    "Save" and "save" are two different ids.
;  * Names are used verbatim: no case folding and no kebab-case rewriting of
;    identifiers by a parser (spec 5.5, 10.1). Identifier *style* is a
;    recommendation (style/README.md), never a validity rule.
;  * Fixed tag vocabularies: type, emotion, status, variant. Tag values are
;    tag-name (lowercase kebab-case). Status tags are initial, translated,
;    reviewed, final.
;  * A field separator is the first "=" or ":" outside a quoted string; the
;    entry anchor is the label "<id>" on its own line, and it runs to the next
;    entry or section line. "<" and ">" carry no other structural meaning and
;    never pair across lines.
;  * A line terminator ("," / ";") is recognized only after the value's
;    closing quote or bracket, is never significant, and is never emitted by a
;    canonical serializer. At most one is permitted per line; a run of them
;    (";;", ",,", ", ;") is a syntax error. A ";" or "," inside a quoted string
;    is payload, and the optional trailing comma inside a list is the list's
;    own production, not a terminator.
;  * Every key appears at most once per scope (header, group, entry);
;    duplicate keys are validity errors — there are no repeatable fields.
;  * Strings containing braces must have balanced braces; the check applies
;    to the fully concatenated value after continuation lines are joined.
;  * List-typed fields (emotion, dependency, reference) are always written
;    as a list, even for a single item; a bare scalar in a list-typed field
;    is a validity error. dependency and reference list items are quoted
;    path/string values.
;  * Continuation lines (string fragments after scalar string fields, bare
;    lists after list fields) concatenate verbatim / extend the list in
;    order. A blank or comment line ends a continuation; an orphan
;    continuation line is a syntax error. Lists themselves never span lines
;    and list items never continue across lines.
;  * Implementations MUST enforce documented, configurable resource limits in
;    both strict and tolerant modes (spec 19): file size, line length, decoded
;    string length, group path segment count, list length, and entry count.
;  * Tolerant parsing (spec Appendix C) is opt-in, is a superset of this
;    grammar, MUST report every repair, and MUST NOT guess the items listed in
;    Appendix C.5. Its output MUST be valid under this grammar.
;  * The identifier relaxations of C.2.5 apply to an identifier that **begins**
;    with a name character (or that uses the quoted form of C.2.4). Angle
;    brackets whose content is not such an identifier are not an entry marker:
;    the line is rejected under C.5, and a tolerant parser MUST NOT manufacture
;    an entry, a group or an identifier out of it, because that would be guessing
;    data the document does not contain. The same rule governs the first segment
;    of a group path inside "[...]". (Appendix C.2.5 gives the cases.)
